# Cloudflare Pages applies these before serving any asset.
#
# ── www → apex ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both hostnames are attached to this one Pages project, so before this rule
# `www.pixelferry.app` answered 200 with the whole site rather than redirecting.
# Every page carries a self-referencing absolute canonical to the apex, so
# search engines were already consolidating on one URL — but two hostnames
# serving one site is still wrong: links, shares and copy-pasted URLs split
# between them, and the duplicate is reachable rather than merely non-canonical.
#
# This lives here, in the repo, rather than as a zone-level Redirect Rule
# because a `_redirects` file is version-controlled, reviewed with the rest of
# the site and verified by `test/redirects.test.ts`. A dashboard rule is none of
# those things. It also needs no zone-edit permission.
#
# `:splat` carries the rest of the path. 301 is permanent and is what a
# canonical host move should be; the apex then applies its own trailing-slash
# and `.html` normalisation as usual, so `www.pixelferry.app/privacy/` lands on
# `https://pixelferry.app/privacy` in two hops rather than one. That is correct
# and only affects a URL nothing should be linking to anyway.
https://www.pixelferry.app/* https://pixelferry.app/:splat 301
